From: lagz0ne <15101444+lagz0ne@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 04:53:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] feat(operator): a token-gated API for users, sharing,
 canvas owners and agent keys

Running a one-person instance with sign-up closed left no way to manage it:
a new account is refused by SIGNUP_EMAIL_DOMAINS, inviting and link access
are owner-only, admin "view as" is read-only, and production without SMTP
promotes no admin at all. Writing the database by hand is not an answer
either: canvas members, owners and link access live in the in-memory store
with write-through, so such a write is invisible until a restart, or
overwritten.

/api/operator (server/operator.ts) is the API a CLI (doopctl) drives, the
way mattermost's mmctl drives its server. It answers only a bearer
DOOP_ADMIN_TOKEN (>= 32 chars, compared in constant time), is absent (404)
while the token is unset, which is the default, and sits ahead of the
session gate, so no account or role is involved. Every write is logged as
"[operator] ...".

- users: list, show, create (sign-up allowlist does not apply, and there is
  no first canvas or demo; a generated password comes back once), set
  password (optionally signing out), ban / unban, delete (?dryRun=1 lists
  the canvases it would delete, same rule as account deletion; agent keys
  go too)
- agent keys for any user: list, mint (secret returned once), revoke
- canvases: list with owner, members, link access; show access; add /
  remove a member by email; set link access; hand over ownership (the
  previous owner stays a member unless keepPrevious is false). Room roles
  are refreshed, so a removed member's open tab loses access at once.

A user is addressed by id or email. auth.ts marks operator creation with an
AsyncLocalStorage, so a browser sign-up running concurrently is still
checked. store.setOwner is new.

tests/operator.test.ts runs it against a real server with sign-up closed
(10 tests); the 40 existing test files that sign users up, plus admin,
agent keys, share links and workspaces, still pass.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
